  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Summer-like conditions through Thursday with highs in the 80s,\n   90s in lower valleys of north-central Idaho.\n\n-  Isolated showers and thunderstorms increase in coverage by\n   Thursday and Friday. \n\n-  Dramatic shift to colder and wetter this weekend. Hypothermia\n   risk. Higher elevations will be \"Winter-like\" with Snow \n   accumulation possible down to 6000-7000 feet.\n\nA subtle disturbance continues to move through western Montana\nearly this morning causing some light showers. The shower activity\nwill diminish by mid morning, then a few storms are anticipated to\ndevelop this afternoon along the Divide.  \n\nTonight into Thursday a pulse of energy will move through the\nregion. Light showers will move through northwest Montana tonight.\nThursday afternoon the atmosphere becomes unstable enough to\nsupport the development of scattered thunderstorms, mainly east of\nthe Highway 93 corridor. These storms will be capable of producing\ngusty erratic winds, brief heavy rain, small hail and lightning.\nThe strength of the storms will diminish after sunset, but the\ncoverage of shower activity will increase Thursday night a\nmoisture and energy from the approaching trough makes its way into\nthe Northern Rockies.\n\nThe cold front is still anticipated to move across the Northern\nRockies on Friday with the trough settling over the region through\nthe weekend. The models are still struggling on how the low will\nactually traverse the region, but anticipate significantly cooler\ntemperatures and widespread precipitation Friday into Monday. The\nfocus of the heaviest precipitation Friday through Saturday will\nbe across north central Idaho into southwest Montana. Then the\nheavier precipitation will transition to northwest Montana,\nespecially along the Divide, Sunday into Monday. Most locations\nwill receive a half to an inch of moisture by Monday afternoon\nwith parts of Glacier National Park getting up to 3 inches of\nmoisture. Snow levels are still anticipated to drop to around\n7000-8000 feet Sunday and Monday mornings allowing for\naccumulating snow in the higher terrain.",
